the main protagonist in this drama is a  u.s. president Donald Trump he's leading  the charge aggressive and unpredictable  his strategy is to exert so much  pressure that the other players make  concessions he announced tariffs on  steel and aluminium imports then  suspended them for the EU and a few  others his declared target is China  which he accuses of technology theft and  unfair trading practices Trump intends  to slap tariffs on 60 billion dollars of  Chinese imports a year it's a risky bet  for many reasons Trump claims the u.s.  is being cheated by other countries in  the realm of global trade his mantras  are America first and make America great  again he's promised to revive  manufacturing at home the US has a  significant Rust Belt regions where the  steel and auto industries among others  collapsed in the face of foreign  competition Chinese President Xi Jinping  is the key counterpart to the  protagonist Trump China wants to avert  an all-out trade war it wants to make  money and enhance its global economic  reach it's presenting itself as a  champion of free trade it said it plans  to impose duties on three billion  dollars worth of imports from the US  including pork Apple's wine and steel  pipes but it's also indicated that it  might consider targeting major players  such as Boeing Apple and Intel  China is the world's largest producer of  steel  exports to the US are hit with tariffs  it will likely seek to boost sales  elsewhere including Europe it's also  possible that the u.s. might threaten to  impose tariffs on clothes and high-tech  goods from China or even do so if China  wants to play hardball and launch a  large-scale retaliation  it might consider slowing its purchases  of US Treasuries it already holds more  US debt than anyone else  a third player is Cecilia malmström the  European Union's trade commissioner in  tough talks with a US administration she  got the tariffs on steel and aluminium  suspended though only temporarily Trump  has set a deadline of May the first to  finalize a deal on trade and once  concessions the EU is seeking to  de-escalate the crisis the last thing it  wants is a trade war a lot is at stake  especially for Germany with its huge  trade surpluses for example it sells a  lot of cars in the United States the  European Union says the deadline of May  the first for a deal is unrealistic  that's when a suspended tariffs are set  to go into force the fight with Trump is  not over yet
 

